  "preamble": "Monophthongization of the rising diphthong [uo] to a long [uː] merges the diphthong with its close onset. A regular case is Kildin Sami, where Proto-Samic *uo became ū before a second-syllable *ē or *ë (*kuolē 'fish' > кӯлль), while it survived as a diphthong before *ā and *ō.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Monophthongization, Raising, Umlaut",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Kildin Sami",
      "languageFamily": "Samic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Samic *kuolē 'fish'",
          "to": "кӯлль, with ӯ [uː]",
          "note": "Kildin monophthongized Proto-Samic *uo to long ū before second-syllable *ē and *ë; before *ā and *ō it gives уэ instead (*kuoltō > куэллт 'winter wind')."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Samic *kuoktē 'two'",
          "to": "кӯһт",
          "note": "Same change; compare Northern Sami guokte."
        },
